<header><p class="enc-kicker">People / group</p><h1 class="enc-title">Estonians</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Estonians are a Finnic people of the eastern Baltic, speaking a language closely related to Finnish, who form the majority of the population of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a>. Organised in independent parishes and counties until the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/northern-crusades">Northern Crusades</a>, they were conquered by German crusaders and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/denmark">Denmark</a> between 1208 and 1227 and lived for centuries under Baltic German landowners within Danish, Swedish and Russian states. A national awakening in the 19th century led to independence in 1918, lost in 1940 and regained in 1991.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/estonians.jpg" alt="Estonians" width="320" height="166" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Allice Hunter · CC BY-SA 4.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Map_of_the_Estonian_Diaspora_in_the_World.svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>Archaeological research shows that the area of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/estonia">Estonia</a> was first inhabited about 13,000 to 11,000 years ago. Some recent linguistic estimates suggest that Finno-Ugric speakers reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a> considerably later, perhaps in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/early-bronze-age">Early Bronze Age</a> around 1800 BCE. It has also been argued that Western Uralic tribes reached Fennoscandia first, giving rise to the Sámi, and arrived in the Baltic region later, which led to the formation of the Baltic Finnic peoples, from whom Estonians and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finns">Finns</a> descend.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The Roman historian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tacitus">Tacitus</a> mentioned the Aesti in 98 CE, and the name is thought to be related to Eesti. The oldest known endonym is maarahvas, meaning land people or country folk, which was used until the middle of the 19th century. The oldest known book in Estonian, the Wanradt–Koell Catechism, was printed in 1535. The ruling elites stayed predominantly German in language and culture after the conquest of the early 13th century.</p><p>Some ethnic awareness preceded the national awakening of the 19th century. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bible">Bible</a> translations appeared in 1739, books and brochures in Estonian rose from 18 in the 1750s to 54 in the 1790s, and by 1800 more than half of adult Estonians could read. The first university-educated intellectuals to identify as Estonians appeared in the 1820s, among them Friedrich Robert Faehlmann, Kristjan Jaak Peterson and Friedrich Reinhold Kreutzwald. By the end of the 1860s Estonians were unwilling to accept German cultural and political hegemony.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Organisation and way of life</h2><p>Estonians speak Estonian, a Finnic language related to Finnish, Karelian and Livonian. Historically and culturally the country divides into Northern and Southern Estonia, and the South Estonian dialects, including Võro and Seto, are often considered a separate language. About 1.1 million ethnic Estonians live worldwide, mostly in Estonia, with diaspora communities in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/finland">Finland</a>, the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/united-states">United States</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sweden">Sweden</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/canada">Canada</a> and the United Kingdom. Large numbers fled over the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/baltic-sea">Baltic Sea</a> in 1944 as the Soviet army occupied Estonia.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Estonians” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Interwar Estonia was a small, mainly rural country of about 1.1 million people, roughly 88 per cent of them ethnic <b>Estonians</b>, with Russian, German, Swedish and Jewish minorities.</blockquote><a href="/empire/republic-of-estonia">Republic of Estonia · Socioeconomy</a></li><li><blockquote>Reval, the medieval name of Tallinn, grew on a site long used by the <b>Estonians</b>, who had a fort on the limestone hill of Toompea and a harbour that the Arab geographer al-Idrisi probably refers to in 1154.</blockquote><a href="/empire/reval">Reval · Foundation</a></li><li><blockquote><b>Estonians</b> defeated invading Swedes in a battle for control of the castle at Lihula, described in the Chronicle of Henry of Livonia and the Livonian Rhymed Chronicle.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/august-8">8 August in history</a></li><li><blockquote>The crusading army of King Valdemar II of Denmark defeated the <b>Estonians</b> of Rävala at Lindanise, the site of present-day Tallinn, and Denmark claimed northern Estonia.</blockquote><a href="/today-in-history/june-15">15 June in history</a></li><li><blockquote>For the first time <b>Estonians</b> governed their own land.</blockquote><a href="/state/autonomous-governorate-of-estonia">Autonomous Governorate of Estonia</a></li><li><blockquote>Hermann of Buxhoeveden, brother of Bishop Albert of Riga, became bishop of the <b>Estonians</b> in 1220 and moved his see to Dorpat after its capture in 1224.</blockquote><a href="/state/bishopric-of-dorpat">Bishopric of Dorpat</a></li></ul></section>
